use super::*;
pub(super) const INTEGER: BuiltinDocumentationSection = BuiltinDocumentationSection{heading:"Integer affine models",paragraphs:&[
            "Declare an integer variable with optimvar(...,'Type','integer',...). Its full original definition, bounds, named dimensions and column-major elements remain in the solve layout, including coordinates whose objective coefficients cancel. The numerical solver orders variable definitions alphabetically by name. Type='semi-continuous' and Type='semi-integer' select zero-or-active-interval domains. Both active bounds must lie in (0,1e5]; zero remains admissible despite a positive active lower bound. Semi-integer active values must be integral. Mixed continuous/discrete affine constraints and objectives select intlinprog; Solver='intlinprog' can also select a compatible continuous affine model. A quadratic or nonlinear model with integer variables is not solved by dropping its integer domains.",
            "A starting-point struct supplies finite values in each original variable shape. It is a repair proposal: the existing integer solver can fix proposed integer values and solve the remaining linear feasibility problem. A rounded proposal is not an incumbent until original feasibility and integrality are verified. Omitted x0 does not create a fabricated initial solution.",
            "The intlinprog options profile consumes AbsoluteGapTolerance, RelativeGapTolerance, ConstraintTolerance, MaxNodes, MaxFeasiblePoints, MaxTime, ObjectiveCutOff, Display, OutputFcn and PlotFcn. Limits, integer-class controls and callback cells retain the same validation as intlinprog. MaxTime uses the selected original Clock during the numerical invocation; it is a cooperative limit, not a hard-preemption guarantee.",
            "Incumbents, pruning bounds and terminal certificates use the original affine expressions and the current branch bounds, not only rounded CSC coefficients. A nonzero stationarity correction without a finite bound supplies no pruning bound. A relaxation ray alone does not establish integer unboundedness. Unrepresentable branch thresholds leave an unresolved numerical stop rather than an artificial variable-domain cap.",
            "Gap, cutoff, certifiedobjective and lowerbound values use the full minimization-oriented original objective: f for minimization and -f for maximization, including its constant. Output/plot callbacks use these same objective units and a flat column vector in the solver ordering; a nonempty callback x represents a newly improving integer-feasible solution. The returned solve fval is always the original f(sol). Exact proof gaps need not equal a subtraction of rounded displayed values.",
            "Integer results return the original named solution structure, fval, a typed exit flag and the integer solver's gap/node/feasible-point information. A stopped feasible point is IntegerFeasible (2); an observer stop is OutputFcnStop (-1). Loss of binary64 publication feasibility is FeasibilityLost (-9). A selected integer solver returns [] for lambda, never the duals of one LP relaxation.",
        ]};
